  const extras = {
    "AI fundamentals": [
      ["AI, machine learning, and deep learning", "AI is the broad goal of making systems perform tasks that usually need human judgment. Machine learning learns patterns from examples; deep learning uses multilayer neural networks to learn increasingly complex representations.", "A spam filter can be machine learning; a vision model that learns image features through many neural-network layers is deep learning."],
      ["Data collection and quality", "AI models learn from data, so collection choices shape what a model can learn and whom it serves. Define the decision first, then gather relevant, permitted, representative data and check missing values, errors, bias, and leakage.", "A support classifier needs representative messages and a reliable label policy; scraping unrelated personal data creates risk without improving the target task."],
      ["Supervised learning", "Supervised learning fits a mapping from examples with known target labels. The model adjusts parameters to reduce the gap between its predictions and the labeled answers.", "Predicting delivery time from route and weather features; classifying a message as spam or not spam."],
      ["Unsupervised learning", "Unsupervised methods look for structure without a provided answer column. They are useful for exploration, compression, and grouping, but a discovered cluster is not automatically meaningful.", "Grouping customers by purchasing behavior; reducing thousands of measurements to a smaller visualization."],
      ["Reinforcement learning", "An agent takes actions in an environment and receives rewards or penalties. Learning seeks a policy that maximizes long-term reward, which can require balancing exploration with reliable behavior.", "A simulated robot learns to reach a target; a recommendation policy tests choices under measured user outcomes."],
      ["Model evaluation and data splits", "Evaluation estimates how well a model will work on data it did not learn from. Keep a test set separate from training and tuning, choose metrics aligned with real costs, and investigate slices rather than relying only on one score.", "For fraud review, precision and recall may matter more than accuracy because fraud is rare and missed cases have unequal cost."],
      ["Overfitting and underfitting", "Overfitting occurs when a model memorizes training-specific details and generalizes poorly. Underfitting occurs when its patterns are too simple to capture the task. Compare training and validation performance before changing model complexity.", "A deep tree with near-perfect training accuracy but weaker validation accuracy may be overfit; a constant predictor underfits."],
      ["Regression, classification, and clustering", "Regression predicts numeric values, classification predicts categories, and clustering groups examples by similarity without known labels. Choose the problem formulation from the decision you need to support, not from the model name.", "Estimate home energy use (regression), flag a transaction category (classification), or explore usage groups (clustering)."],
      ["AI ethics and responsible use", "Responsible AI considers privacy, fairness, transparency, safety, and accountability across data collection, training, deployment, and monitoring. Human review and clear escalation paths are important when mistakes affect people.", "Audit error rates across relevant groups before deploying a high-impact classifier; collect only data needed for the stated purpose."]
    ],
    "Python": [
      ["Python variables and types", "A variable is a name bound to a value. Python values have types such as int, float, bool, and str; types determine available operations. Prefer descriptive names and validate data at boundaries.", "A price is usually numeric, while a product identifier may be a string even when it contains digits."],
      ["Python strings", "Strings are immutable sequences of Unicode text. Indexing and slicing produce new strings; methods such as strip, split, and replace return transformed values rather than changing the original.", "Normalize a user-entered name with strip before comparing it; keep the original value when exact input is needed for auditing."],
      ["Lists and list methods", "A list is an ordered, mutable collection. Use append to add one item, extend to add many, and sort to change its order in place. Be careful when changing a list while iterating over it.", "A batch of prompts can be stored in a list and processed in sequence."],
      ["Tuples and dictionaries", "A tuple is an ordered immutable collection, useful for fixed records. A dictionary maps hashable keys to values and is useful for named lookup; use get or explicit membership checks for optional keys.", "Represent a point as (x, y); store a user record as a dictionary with keys such as name and email."],
      ["Conditionals and loops", "Conditionals select a path based on a Boolean expression. for loops iterate over values; while loops repeat while a condition remains true. Prefer for loops for finite collections and ensure while loops make progress toward termination.", "Retry a transient request a bounded number of times, then report failure rather than retrying forever."],
      ["Functions and modules", "Functions package a named operation with inputs and an optional result. Modules organize reusable Python code. Small functions with clear inputs, outputs, and side effects are easier to test and combine.", "A parse_record function can validate one row and return a structured result; a separate module can hold reusable parsing logic."],
      ["List comprehensions and lambdas", "A list comprehension constructs a list from an iterable with an optional filter. A lambda is a small anonymous function, most useful as a short key or callback; use a named def when logic needs explanation.", "Extract normalized names with [name.strip() for name in names] instead of manually appending in a loop."],
      ["Object-oriented programming", "OOP groups state and behavior into objects created from classes. Use it when a domain has meaningful entities and invariants; do not force a class around simple data transformations that are clearer as functions.", "A Dataset class may validate records at construction and expose safe iteration over them."],
      ["Jupyter notebooks", "A notebook combines executable code cells with explanatory text and output. Cells can run out of order, so restart and run all cells before sharing; keep important reusable logic in tested Python modules.", "Use a notebook to inspect sample data and plot distributions, then move production transformations to a module."]
    ],
    "NLP": [
      ["Text preprocessing and tokenization", "Preprocessing converts raw text into a representation suitable for a task. Tokenization splits text into units; the right choice depends on language and model. Keep task-relevant signals such as punctuation when they carry meaning.", "A search index may normalize case, while sentiment analysis may preserve exclamation marks."],
      ["Stop words, stemming, and lemmatization", "Stop-word removal drops chosen frequent terms; stemming heuristically truncates words, while lemmatization maps them to dictionary forms. These steps can help sparse classical models but may erase meaning and often are not appropriate for modern language models.", "The words 'was' and 'be' may be lemmatized together; a negation such as 'not' should not be casually removed."],
      ["POS tagging and named entities", "Part-of-speech tagging assigns grammatical roles to tokens. Named entity recognition detects spans such as people, organizations, and places. Both are model predictions and should be checked for domain-specific errors.", "A contract analysis tool can identify organization names for review, not treat extraction as verified legal truth."],
      ["Bag of Words and TF-IDF", "Bag of Words represents text using token counts and ignores word order. TF-IDF downweights terms common across documents and highlights terms distinctive to a document. Both are efficient baselines for many text-classification tasks.", "Compare TF-IDF features with a simple linear classifier before adopting a more costly model."],
      ["Embeddings and semantic similarity", "An embedding maps text to a numeric vector where useful semantic relationships can be reflected in geometry. Similarity search ranks nearby vectors, but relevance depends on the embedding model, text preparation, and evaluation data.", "Find policy passages that are semantically related to a user's question even when they use different wording."],
      ["Text classification and topic modeling", "Text classification predicts known categories using labeled examples. Topic modeling explores recurring word patterns without predefined labels. Predicted classes need evaluation; exploratory topics need human interpretation.", "Route support tickets into known queues; use topic clusters to discover emerging product complaints."],
      ["Transformer-based NLP", "Transformers use attention to combine information across token positions. They process context more flexibly than earlier sequential architectures and are the basis of many current language models, though long contexts cost more computation.", "A transformer can use surrounding words to distinguish 'bank' as a river edge from a financial institution."]
    ],
    "Deep learning": [
      ["Neural networks and backpropagation", "A neural network applies layers of learned transformations and nonlinear activation functions. During training, a loss measures prediction error; backpropagation computes gradients, and an optimizer updates parameters to reduce that loss.", "An image classifier maps pixel tensors through learned layers to class scores; monitor validation loss to detect overfitting."],
      ["Neurons, layers, and activation functions", "A neural unit combines inputs using learned weights and a bias, then applies an activation function. Layers compose these operations; nonlinear activations let a network represent more than a single linear transformation.", "ReLU is commonly used in hidden layers; the output layer and activation should fit the task, such as class probabilities or a numeric estimate."],
      ["Forward propagation and backpropagation", "Forward propagation computes a prediction by passing inputs through the network. A loss compares that prediction with the target; backpropagation applies the chain rule to calculate gradients used by an optimizer to update weights.", "During training, inspect whether loss improves on training and held-out validation data rather than assuming each update helps."],
      ["CNNs, RNNs, and LSTMs", "Convolutional neural networks share local filters and work well for spatial patterns. Recurrent networks process sequences step by step; LSTMs add gates to retain or forget information over longer sequences. Transformers are often preferred for large-scale sequence modeling today.", "A CNN detects local image features; an LSTM can model time series when sequential state is useful."]
    ],
    "Generative AI and LLMs": [
      ["Tokens, tokenization, and context windows", "A language model reads token IDs, not words exactly. Tokenization can split rare words into pieces; the context window limits how much input and generated text fit in one request. Count tokens rather than characters when managing limits.", "A long document may exceed a model's context even if it looks short after compression."],
      ["Attention and transformer architecture", "Self-attention computes how strongly each token should use information from other tokens. Transformer blocks combine attention and feed-forward transformations; training learns parameters from large text collections.", "In 'The animal did not cross the road because it was tired,' attention can connect 'it' with the earlier noun."],
      ["Pretraining, fine-tuning, and foundation models", "Pretraining learns broad patterns from large corpora. Fine-tuning further adapts a model using curated examples for a narrower behavior. Foundation models can be reused across tasks but still require task-specific evaluation and safety controls.", "Start with a hosted foundation model for a prototype, then fine-tune only if measured gaps justify the data and maintenance cost."],
      ["Prompt engineering and model parameters", "A prompt provides instructions, context, and output constraints. Temperature affects sampling diversity, not factual accuracy; lower temperature can make output more consistent but cannot guarantee correctness. Validate structured outputs before use.", "Ask for JSON with a schema, parse it, and reject invalid fields instead of assuming formatting instructions always work."],
      ["Selecting a language model", "Model selection is a measured trade-off across task quality, latency, cost, data handling, availability, and deployment needs. Test candidate models against the same representative task set and review their terms before choosing.", "A smaller model may be sufficient for routing requests, while a more capable model may be reserved for harder questions."],
      ["Hallucinations and grounding", "A hallucination is fluent output that is unsupported or incorrect. Retrieval, citations, constrained tools, and abstention can reduce some failures but do not remove the need to verify outputs against trusted sources.", "For policy questions, retrieve approved passages and show citations; return 'not found' when evidence is absent."]
    ],
    "RAG and vector search": [
      ["RAG architecture", "Retrieval-augmented generation retrieves relevant source material and includes it with a user query before generation. A typical flow is ingest → chunk → embed → index → retrieve → construct context → generate → verify.", "A support assistant retrieves product documentation and answers with links to the source passages."],
      ["Chunking and document ingestion", "Ingestion extracts and normalizes source content; chunking divides it into retrievable units. Chunk size and overlap trade retrieval precision against context coverage. Preserve metadata such as document, page, and update time.", "Split a handbook by headings and keep section names on each chunk so retrieved text retains context."],
      ["Vector databases and similarity search", "A vector index stores embeddings and searches for nearby vectors using a chosen distance or similarity measure. Metadata filters and exact keyword search can complement vector ranking; benchmark on representative queries.", "Filter retrieval to the current product version before ranking passages by semantic similarity."],
      ["Retrieval and context construction", "Retrieval selects candidate evidence, while context construction deduplicates, ranks, and fits passages into the model budget. Poor retrieval cannot be repaired reliably by a persuasive prompt.", "Rerank top candidates and include source identifiers, then leave room for the answer within the context limit."],
      ["RAG evaluation and optimization", "Evaluate retrieval relevance separately from answer correctness and groundedness. Use representative question-answer evidence pairs, inspect failure cases, and change one stage at a time: parsing, chunking, embeddings, filters, ranking, or generation.", "Measure recall at k for known supporting passages and check whether final answers cite the right passage."]
    ],
    "AI agents": [
      ["AI agent architecture and tools", "An AI agent uses a model to select actions, often by calling bounded tools and observing results. Tools should have narrow permissions, validated inputs, timeouts, and clear error handling; a model's proposal is not authorization.", "A read-only calendar assistant can search available slots, present options, and ask before creating an event."],
      ["Agent memory, planning, and workflows", "Memory stores selected context across steps; planning breaks a goal into actions; workflows constrain transitions and handoffs. Persist only necessary data, cap loops, and make progress observable.", "A research workflow can search sources, summarize evidence, and request a human review before publishing."],
      ["Multi-agent systems", "A multi-agent system coordinates multiple model-driven roles or workers. It can separate tasks, but adds communication cost, coordination failures, duplicated work, and harder evaluation; use it only when a simpler single-agent workflow is insufficient.", "One bounded worker can retrieve evidence while another checks a draft against cited source passages; a controller can stop when evidence is missing."],
      ["Human review and agent evaluation", "Human-in-the-loop controls let a person approve consequential actions or resolve uncertainty. Evaluate agents on task success, tool correctness, policy compliance, cost, and recovery from tool failures—not only on fluent text.", "Require explicit confirmation before sending an email, and test that the agent handles a denied permission safely."]
    ],
    "AI engineering": [
      ["APIs, deployment, latency, and cost", "AI applications call model or data services through APIs. Production design should handle timeouts, retries with limits, rate limits, privacy, monitoring, and fallback behavior. Measure end-to-end latency and per-task cost against actual usage.", "Cache stable embeddings, stream long responses when useful, and log token usage without storing sensitive prompt contents unnecessarily."],
      ["Calling a model API from an application", "A model API request typically sends an instruction and input, then receives generated output and usage metadata. Keep credentials outside source code, set timeouts, handle service errors, and validate output before downstream use.", "A Python service can submit a request to a configured provider, parse the result, and return a constrained response to its caller."],
      ["OpenAI API integration", "Integrating a hosted model API means sending a bounded request from an application and handling the structured response. Store credentials outside source code, review data-handling requirements, set timeouts and usage limits, and validate model output before acting on it.", "A server-side application can read a credential from its environment, call a selected model through the provider's supported client, and return only validated fields to its user interface."],
      ["Hugging Face, open source, and frameworks", "Hugging Face hosts model and dataset tooling; open-source models may offer control and local deployment but require infrastructure, licensing review, and operations. Frameworks can speed orchestration but should not obscure the underlying data flow.", "Prototype a retrieval pipeline with a framework, then test whether the dependency is still useful at deployment scale."],
      ["AI projects and system design", "An AI application combines a user problem, data pipeline, model, evaluation, interface, and operational safeguards. Start with a baseline and measurable acceptance criteria, then ship a small slice and monitor real failure modes.", "A document assistant needs permission-aware ingestion, retrieval tests, source-linked answers, feedback capture, and a deletion process."]
    ]
  };

